            <ArticleTitle>Cognitive Complexity, Attitudinal Affect, and Dispersion in Affect Ratings for Products.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>209-212</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/00224545.1979.9922700</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The purpose of this study was to examine the relationships between cognitive complexity, attitudinal affect, and dispersion of affect scores (N = 102 male business administration undergraduates). Models of automobiles and toothpaste brands were the content domains studied. Analysis using Pearson product-moment correlation supported the hypothesis that cognitive complex Ss had a lower level of affect and greater dispersion of affect scores than did simpler Ss.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
